<Lesson slug="shared-understanding">
  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Meet the repair café</h2>
    <div class="scene">
      <p>A repair café meets in the town library on the first Saturday of every month. People bring broken things such as lamps, toasters, jeans and bikes, and volunteer repairers fix them alongside the owner. You are on the small team that builds software for the café.</p>
      <p>Last Saturday a visitor called Sam carried a broken lamp across town and queued for forty minutes. At the front of the queue, Sam learned that Ade, the only volunteer who repairs electrical items, was away that day. Sam took the lamp home again. The sewing bench stood empty all morning.</p>
      <p>On Monday the café’s coordinator, Noor, sends the team a one-line request: <strong>“We need a booking calendar.”</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Same words, different pictures</h2>
    <p>Three people read Noor’s line. Open each card to see what that person imagines.</p>
    <div class="reveal">
      <details>
        <summary><span>Noor, the coordinator</span></summary>
        <p>A page that shows which repairers are in on which Saturday. Visitors look at it before they leave home. Nobody books anything.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><span>A developer on your team</span></summary>
        <p>Appointment slots, visitor accounts, reminder emails and a way to cancel. It looks like about three months of work.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary><span>Sam, the visitor</span></summary>
        <p>Nothing about calendars. Sam wants to know one thing before leaving home: will someone be able to fix a lamp this Saturday?</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <p>All three would agree with the sentence, and each pictures something different. Jeff Patton, the author of <cite>User Story Mapping</cite>, sums up the problem in one line.</p>
    <blockquote class="pull"><p>Shared documents aren’t shared understanding.</p></blockquote>
    <p>A longer and more careful document would not fix this. Readers fill the gaps in any document with their own assumptions, and nobody can see that they have done it. The differences come out later, when the wrong thing has been built.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>What a story is</h2>
    <p>A <dfn>user story</dfn> is a short description of something a person wants to do, written on a card so that the team remembers to talk about it. Stories are named for how you use them, which is by telling them. The card is small because the telling is where the work happens.</p>
    <p>Ron Jeffries, one of the people who shaped the practice, described a story as three parts.</p>
    <dl class="terms">
      <div><dt>Card</dt><dd>A few words that name the story. They are enough to plan with and to remind you of the conversation.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Conversation</dt><dd>The people who need the thing and the people who will build it talk it through, with sketches and examples, until they picture the same thing.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Confirmation</dt><dd>They agree how they will check that it is done.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>You may know the template “As a visitor, I want to book a slot so that I don’t make a wasted trip.” It is a good way to open the conversation, because it makes you say who, what and why. A card in that format that nobody has discussed is no better than Noor’s one-line request.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Keep what you talked about</h2>
    <p>Conversations evaporate, so record what you agree while you talk: notes on cards, a sketch, a photo of the board. Patton compares these records to holiday photos. A photo brings the whole trip back for the people who were there, and means far less to anyone who was not. Someone who missed the conversation needs to hear the story told again, with the record in hand.</p>
    <p>The rest of this course builds a map out of cards for that reason. A map gives a group something to point at while they tell the story together.</p>
  </section>
</Lesson>
